20.1  From firmware to init

TL;DR
  - The firmware (UEFI or BIOS) checks the hardware and starts a bootloader from
    disk.
  - The bootloader (GRUB, systemd-boot) loads the kernel and the initramfs into
    RAM.
  - The kernel uses the initramfs to find and mount the real root filesystem,
    then starts init: process 1.
  - Init (systemd on most distros) starts services until it reaches the default
    target.

      systemd-analyze          # time spent in each stage
      systemd-analyze blame    # slowest services first
      journalctl -b -1         # logs of the previous boot

On your machine
  Firmware  UEFI · Secure Boot on
  Kernel    6.12.48-amd64
  Cmdline   BOOT_IMAGE=/vmlinuz-6.12.48-amd64
            root=UUID=3f1c2a9e-8b7d-4e21-9c55-0a6b2d7e9f10 ro rootflags=subvol=@
            quiet splash
  Init      systemd · default target graphical.target
  Loader    systemd-boot 256.4 · 1 unified kernel image(s)
  Up        3 days, 4 h

Pitfalls
  - journalctl -b -1 needs a persistent journal (/var/log/journal exists).
    Otherwise the logs of earlier boots are gone.
  - With Secure Boot on, only signed bootloaders, kernels and modules load: a
    module built on your machine (NVIDIA through DKMS, for one) has to be signed
    with a key you enroll.
  - A broken initramfs drops you into an emergency shell before root is mounted.
    Rebuild it from a live USB with your distro's tool: update-initramfs, dracut
    or mkinitcpio.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 20.1 --why

